{"signature": "6fe08f0df0f76472ea30b7b054ccbf1224ebec89b8f00df6d856896ed7eced9d", "result": {"checks": {"check": {"scope": {"id": "check", "service": "confluence", "file": "confluence_working.json", "pointer": "/pages", "field": "id", "values": ["page_domino_gdd", "page_live_ops_engine"]}, "matches": [{"file": "HarmonyGames_Other_Services/Services_Data/confluence/confluence_working.json", "pointer": "/pages/6", "field": "id", "value": "page_live_ops_engine", "record": {"id": "page_live_ops_engine", "space_id": "space_ENG", "parent_id": "page_eng_home", "title": "Live-Ops Event Engine", "body": "<p>The Live-Ops Event Engine powers all recurring in-game events for Zombie Match 3D (listed as \"Puzzles vs Zombies\" until the July 2025 store rename). It defines, schedules, and fires timed content - <strong>Plague Race</strong>, Zombie Lane, Endless Monster Mode - and drives the associated leaderboards and lives economy. This page documents the architecture, the scheduler's known fragility, and the July 2025 outage that took <strong>Domino Delights</strong> live events dark - the record never marks it restored. For upcoming cadence and slot ownership see <ac:link><ri:page ri:space-key=\"OPS\" ri:content-title=\"Live-Ops Event Calendar &amp; Planning\"/><ac:link-body>Live-Ops Event Calendar &amp; Planning</ac:link-body></ac:link>.</p><h2>Architecture</h2><p>The engine has three cooperating parts. Event <em>definitions</em> are reward tables authored by design in Google Sheets and hot-loaded by the backend; the <strong>scheduler</strong> is the runtime that evaluates windows and fires state transitions; the leaderboard service ranks entries and settles rewards at event close.</p><table><tbody><tr><th>Component</th><th>Responsibility</th><th>Owner</th></tr><tr><td>Event definitions</td><td>Reward tables are authored in Google Sheets and imported hourly; windows are cron expressions baked into the CDK stack; there is no remote kill-switch for live events - Domino Delights gates them on a compiled client constant and Zombie Match 3D's per-event flags are literals in the backend runtime config, so turning an event on or off means a deploy or a direct database edit. There is no cohort-targeting field.</td><td>Design (Julia Lawson)</td></tr><tr><td>Scheduler</td><td>Each event type runs its own cron, all at 00:00 UTC - Plague Race, Endless Monster daily and Boss Arena daily; Leagues, Endless Monster weekly and Collect &amp; Win weekly; Season Pass monthly on the 1st at 00:00 UTC (the CDK sets its schedule to <code>0 0 1 * *</code> in production and the module is the monthly season-pass server); each season then runs to the start of the next month, which is the monthly window the Season Pass spec describes). The Quantum scheduler runs both: the four Google-Sheets reward importers (hourly in production) and every event-reset job - each leaderboard server registers its own Quantum job at boot from the cron string on its leaderboard row, so event firing and reward import share one scheduler. Lives regeneration is not on any of these.</td><td>Backend (Douglas)</td></tr><tr><td>Leaderboard service</td><td>Ingests scores during a window, freezes and settles rewards at close</td><td>Backend</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Lives regeneration</h3><p>Life regeneration is computed <strong>client-side</strong> from server time and written back to the server; the server only clamps negative values. It is not on the scheduler and shares no failure domain with event firing - the two are independent. The July 2025 outage saw both symptoms at once, but that was co-occurrence: the stuck-lives reports are a separate, still-open client-side bug (ENG-2277, ENG-2400). See the incident below.</p><ac:structured-macro ac:name=\"warning\"><ac:rich-text-body><p><strong>Scheduler has no leader: every node runs its own.</strong> The scheduler is a supervised in-process Quantum instance on EVERY node, not a cluster-wide singleton: jobs register with a node-local run strategy while production runs three Fargate tasks, so each event-reset cron currently fires once per task. A crashed process is restarted in place by its own supervisor; what it does not survive is a deploy that leaves the job unscheduled. A single stalled process does not stop event firing; what does is a deploy that leaves the job unscheduled on every node, which is how the July 2025 stall happened. Lives are unaffected - they are computed on the client. Do not add new subsystems to the tick loop without a leader-election plan.</p></ac:rich-text-body></ac:structured-macro><h2>July 2025 outage</h2><p>From at least <strong>Jul 11, 2025</strong> the event scheduler stopped advancing for <strong>Domino Delights</strong>, and no message anywhere in the record marks it restored. The same failure had happened a fortnight earlier and was cleared by hand. Lives are computed client-side (see above) and are not on the scheduler, so the stall could not stop the timer. The stuck-lives reports in the same thread are a separate, still-open bug (ENG-2277, ENG-2400) whose cause was never established. The lives write path stayed up throughout - backend granted manual lives top-ups on 14, 19 and 24 July while events were still not firing - and other accounts regenerated normally on the same build. The earliest symptom in the July thread is missing weekend events, reported on <strong>Jul 12, 2025</strong> alongside a lives timer stuck at four. The <strong>03:41 on Jul 13</strong> figure is one reported player's lives balance beginning to fall in Metabase, not a first observation - a ticket relayed on Jul 12 said the problem had already been running for three days. Players reported seeing only two live events on the weekend, with <strong>River Rush</strong>, Streak Fever and Axe Arena not firing. It was <em>not</em> global - other accounts regenerated lives normally throughout - and no end date exists in the record: the same player's lives were broken again on Jul 24, coin balances were reported stuck negative on Jul 28, the same events-not-firing-until-someone-triggers-the-scheduler-by-hand failure was still being reported on Oct 27, and a lives-regeneration bug (ENG-2400) opened on Nov 2, 2025 was never closed. Zombie Match 3D was unaffected; its Plague Race ran normally that week. No standalone ticket was cut at the time; the timeline was reconstructed afterwards from support threads and scheduler logs.</p><ac:structured-macro ac:name=\"info\"><ac:rich-text-body><p>Measure this outage from <code>LIVE_EVENT_PERFORMANCE</code> status and participation on Domino Delights, not from a revenue delta. Domino Delights revenue declined steadily across Jun-Jul 2025 as part of that title's wind-down, before, during and after the window; do not read that trend as outage impact.</p></ac:rich-text-body></ac:structured-macro><h3>Remediation</h3><ul><li>Add a heartbeat + external watchdog that pages on a missed scheduler tick (target: alert within 5 minutes, not 12+ hours).</li><li>Monitor the lives write-back route independently of scheduler ticks, so two co-occurring symptoms in different failure domains can be told apart on the first day rather than the third.</li><li>Introduce leader election so each cron fires once cluster-wide instead of once per Fargate task (Quantum.RunStrategy.Global, or an external lock).</li><li>Build a repro harness for ENG-2277 / ENG-2400 - every reported case was cleared only by a manual backend lives grant, and no fix was ever shipped.</li><li>Dashboard the scheduler's last-tick timestamp alongside revenue so drift is visible on the same screen.</li></ul><p>Design context for the lives and event-timing models and the related quest timing regression lives in <ac:link><ri:page ri:content-title=\"Quest System \u2014 Design &amp; Incident Postmortem\"/><ac:link-body>Quest System - Design &amp; Incident Postmortem</ac:link-body></ac:link>.</p>", "body_format": "storage", "status": "current", "version": 3, "author_id": "user_EMPLOYEE_0031", "created_at": "2024-08-15T16:07:58Z", "updated_at": "2025-11-30T03:33:48Z"}}, {"file": "HarmonyGames_Other_Services/Services_Data/confluence/confluence_working.json", "pointer": "/pages/15", "field": "id", "value": "page_domino_gdd", "record": {"id": "page_domino_gdd", "space_id": "space_PROD", "parent_id": "page_prod_home", "title": "Domino Delights \u2014 Game Design Document", "body": "<p><strong>Domino Delights</strong> (internal codename <em>\"Game of Dominoes\"</em> / GoD) is Harmony Games' tile-connecting puzzle title with light-RPG progression. This document is the living design reference for the core loop, character system, and retention mini-games. Store title is <strong>Domino Delights</strong>; use <em>Game of Dominoes</em> or <strong>GoD</strong> internally.</p><ac:structured-macro ac:name=\"info\"><ac:rich-text-body><p>Built through the <strong>Baklava</strong> release train, which shipped 11 September 2024; the soft launch itself was the US go-live on <strong>11 April 2025</strong>, worldwide on 6 May 2025. ~4,755 levels across 160 chapters are in production. This GDD supersedes the pre-soft-launch draft; see the version history for the edit trail.</p></ac:rich-text-body></ac:structured-macro><h2>Core Loop &amp; Mechanics</h2><p>Players match a face-up board tile against the tile in hand &mdash; tripeak-solitaire structure on domino tiles &mdash; drawing from the draw pile when nothing matches. Each unbroken match chain feeds a streak meter; sustained combos drive the light-RPG layer, where collected characters level up and unlock stronger board-clearing abilities. A single run alternates between board-clearing turns and character-ability triggers, keeping session length short but replayable across the ~4,755-level ladder.</p><h3>Boosts</h3><ul><li><strong>Magical Gust</strong> - a wind boost that clears all face-up tiles on the board, resetting a stalled position.</li><li>Character abilities (below) act as targeted boosts that recharge through matching.</li></ul><p>For coin, life, and booster balancing that these mechanics feed into, see <ac:link><ri:page ri:content-title=\"Economy Design \u2014 Coins, Lives &amp; Boosters\"/><ac:link-body>Economy Design - Coins, Lives &amp; Boosters</ac:link-body></ac:link>.</p><h2>Characters</h2><p>Four playable characters (Sophie is the starter, the other three are collectible), each with its own upgrade track. Leveling a character deepens its signature effect and reduces its recharge cost.</p><table><tbody><tr><th>Character</th><th>Signature ability</th><th>Upgrade track</th></tr><tr><td>Sophie</td><td>Starts unlocked. Adds tiles to the draw pile and turns tiles wild</td><td>Improves draw quality and wild frequency</td></tr><tr><td>Nathan</td><td>Removes a set of random face-up tiles; jumbo tile to hand</td><td>Increases tiles removed per trigger and jumbo size</td></tr><tr><td>Julius</td><td>Removes face-up tiles and converts them to gold or wild</td><td>Increases tiles converted per trigger</td></tr><tr><td>Lexi</td><td>Foresight - reveals upcoming tiles</td><td>Extends how far ahead she reveals</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Wildcard is a <em>booster</em> (1,600 coins), not a character.</p><ac:structured-macro ac:name=\"note\"><ac:rich-text-body><p>Character names and the fifth slot are provisional. Do not surface upgrade numbers in marketing until the economy pass locks recharge costs.</p></ac:rich-text-body></ac:structured-macro><h2>Retention Mini-Games</h2><p>Between level runs, players can enter reward mini-games that keep engagement high on off-days:</p><ul><li><strong>Tile Dozer</strong> - a coin-pusher where dropped tiles shove a stacked tray toward the edge for coin and booster payouts.</li><li><strong>Lucky Drop</strong> - a drop-and-bounce board awarding tiered rewards by landing slot (prototyped as \"Plinko\"; renamed for trademark reasons before ship).</li><li><strong>River Tile</strong> - a timed <em>matching</em> mini-game (prototyped as \"Conveyor Belt\").</li><li><strong>Memory Game</strong> - a pairs game on a timer.</li></ul><p>Reward scaling for <strong>Tile Dozer</strong> and the other mini-games is tuned to daily-return targets and shares the coin sink with the core economy.</p><h2>Competitive Context</h2><p>Positioning benchmarks against the tile-matching leader are tracked in <ac:link><ri:page ri:content-title=\"Competitive Analysis \u2014 Match Factory\"/><ac:link-body>Competitive Analysis - Match Factory</ac:link-body></ac:link>.</p>", "body_format": "storage", "status": "current", "version": 4, "author_id": "user_EMPLOYEE_0016", "created_at": "2023-06-01T09:12:58Z", "updated_at": "2025-09-15T14:39:52Z"}}], "records_checked": 31, "files_selected": 1, "files_completed": 1, "scan_complete": true, "errors": []}}, "files_read_this_call": 0, "sources": [{"file": "HarmonyGames_Other_Services/Services_Data/confluence/confluence_working.json", "manifest_sha256": "e6840608051cf49e37aeb94a719fed78e0d38eb902d30f86805b84ea0d67adb6", "freshness": "970c4a92af37d08dd66b69b75daa8db99f043a7078d21e855c130a5f6081383c"}], "scan_complete": true, "persona_filtered": false, "backend": "record-check-cache", "scope_note": "Exact direct fields in selected arrays. No SQL is applied. Absence/uniqueness is supported only within complete declared scopes. Manifest hashes are declared provenance, not fresh full-content hashes.", "checks_reused": ["g0"]}, "checksum": "0b0cba0a65815ffa281d8cdf9d5ec213b5f6f1ff53bdbfa4ae6217a8b325a977"}